WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.920
As you may know, if you've been paying attention
to the news for the last few days, votes are

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.010
still being counted in the 2020 presidential
election and Joe Biden's lead over Donald

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.980
Trump has now actually surpassed five million
votes.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.519
And this is, I think, a really good time to
add some context to these numbers, because

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:24.369
depending on what historical context you prefer
to add, the sort of takeaways from Joe Biden's

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:27.609
current popular vote win would be pretty dramatically
different.

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:33.120
So with Joe Biden at close to seventy seven
million votes and Donald Trump at close to

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:41.360
72 million votes, the exact lead for Joe Biden
as of moments ago was about five point zero

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.390
seven million votes.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.250
In other words, five million plus seventy
thousand votes.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:51.829
And when you look back at the history of presidential
elections, this is not even close to the largest

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:52.829
winning margin.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:57.410
If you're looking at some of the historical
landslides, for example, if you look at Richard

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:05.819
Nixon's historic 17 million vote margin in
1972, if you look at Ronald Reagan's in 84

00:01:05.819 --> 00:01:14.119
by 16 million votes, LBJ and FDR won by 15
and 11 million votes, respectively.

00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:19.090
Even Barack Obama won by more than nine million
votes in 2008.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.240
But needless to say, this is a healthy margin
for Joe Biden.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:30.130
And all of the huge wins that I mentioned
just now came with far larger electoral victories

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.130
as well.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.479
And that's something not to be ignored.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:39.609
Now, a really interesting fact is that among
the largest popular vote losses among candidates

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:45.079
who won, in other words, popular vote lost
won the presidency electorally.

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:50.679
Donald Trump's popular vote loss in 2016,
which was, as you remember, by about three

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:58.829
million votes to Hillary Clinton, is the largest
popular vote loss in history for a candidate

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:00.790
who won the Electoral College.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:06.189
And it's followed by George W. Bush losing
the popular vote by about half a million in

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:11.341
the year 2000 to Al Gore and still winning
electorally, as you remember, Florida, the

00:02:11.341 --> 00:02:13.770
decider in that particular election.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:20.340
For me, the most interesting perspective on
the numbers so far is that Joe Biden has fifty

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.580
point eight percent of the vote right now.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.020
And when you look at challengers, which accounts
for the power of incumbency, remember, removing

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:33.400
an incumbent president is really difficult,
even an unpopular one, 2004.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.950
And George W. Bush's reelection is a strong
reminder of that among presidential challengers

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.060
to an incumbent.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.680
Joe Biden's fifty point eight percent, which
may still go up, is the highest vote share

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.310
for a challenger to an incumbent president
dating all the way back to Franklin Delano

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:57.470
Roosevelt in 1932, who got 57 percent of the
vote as the challenger in 32.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:01.410
And in fact, it's higher at fifty point eight.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:07.510
Joe Biden's share of the vote is higher even
than Ronald Reagan's, which was fifty point

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.800
seven in 1980.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:15.180
So needless to say, it's a very healthy win,
popular vote.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.850
And yet it came down to a combined razor thin
margin of, you know, we're waiting on the

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.850
Georgia results.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:27.080
But as of right now, we're talking about 70000
votes in Nevada and Pennsylvania.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.510
Or you could put it a different way and say
Joe Biden won thanks to sixty thousand votes

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.400
in Pennsylvania and Arizona, depending on
what you consider the winning states for Joe

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.400
Biden.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.670
So what can we say?

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.130
More generally?

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:45.280
We can say that Donald Trump's two elections
taken together are remarkable.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:52.190
Trump lost the popular vote twice, is one
of only 10 incumbents to lose reelection and

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.380
one of only three presidents to be impeached.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.670
And if you sort of make a Venn diagram of
this, which people have done and have shared

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:04.590
on social media, if you take this together,
Donald Trump is the only president to lose

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:10.370
the popular vote twice, be removed after one
term and be impeached.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:14.790
So that's quite a resume for him now.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.590
Is there a bigger story than just trivia?

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.810
There is, but it really depends on your perspective.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:26.930
So on the one hand, how did Joe Biden manage
to almost lose the Electoral College in the

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.410
middle of the worst president in the modern
eras, globally embarrassing handling of a

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.020
100 year pandemic?

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.950
Joe Biden almost didn't win.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.190
That's not good.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:45.430
On the other hand, even with Republican voter
suppression, Republican voter purges trumps

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.530
election disinformation, attempted sabotage
of the postal service.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:56.840
Even with all of that, Joe Biden still won
by more than five million votes and may end

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.540
up with the exact number of.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:04.430
Rural votes 306, as Donald Trump had in 2016,
depending on what ultimately happens in Georgia.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.940
My view is both of these things are true.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:15.470
We have to be a what what right wing pseudo
intellectual said reality of the world is

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:17.430
messy or something like that.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.630
We can hold both ideas at the same time.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:24.240
We have to take the wins where we got them,
which is that Joe Biden won, period.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.240
And we have to now improve going forward.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.280
We have to pour everything we have into the
two Senate runoff races in Georgia, which

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.270
we will be talking about quite a bit tomorrow,
and realize that it was close when it came

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:41.450
down to not the five million votes, but the
under 100000 votes that the winning states

00:05:41.450 --> 00:07:39.090
ended up depending on.

